This section presents a systematic empirical study of the relevance-diversity trade-off in visual token compression. We first formulate the dataset-level optimal relevance-diversity weight and identify how it varies across benchmarks. To gain deeper insight into the origins of this cross-dataset variation, we further investigate the distribution of instance-level optimal weights within each benchmark. Since no continuous metric directly reflects token selection quality at the sample level, we introduce per-sample answer likelihood as a surrogate objective to recover these instance-level weights. Based on both levels of analysis, we report empirical observations that motivate the design of our method. Dataset-Level Optimal Weight. Given a multimodal input with visual token set V = { v 1 , … , v N } V=\{v_{1},\ldots,v_{N}\} and text token sequence T T , we define the per-token selection score as a weighted combination of text relevance Rel ⁡ ( ⋅ , T ) \mathrm{Rel}(\cdot,T) and visual diversity Div ⁡ ( ⋅ ) \mathrm{Div}(\cdot) : (1) Score θ ​ ( v j , T ) = ( 1 − θ ) ⋅ Rel ⁡ ( v j , T ) + θ ⋅ Div ⁡ ( v j ) , \mathrm{Score}_{	heta}(v_{j},\,T)=(1-	heta)\cdot\mathrm{Rel}(v_{j},\,T)\;+\;	heta\cdot\mathrm{Div}(v_{j}), where θ ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] 	heta\in[0,1] controls the relevance-diversity balance, with larger θ 	heta corresponding to greater diversity weight. Under a fixed retention budget K K , we select the top- K K tokens by score: (2) V θ = Top K ​ { Score θ ​ ( v j , T ) } j = 1 N . V_{	heta}=\mathrm{Top}_{K} igl\{\mathrm{Score}_{	heta}(v_{j},\,T) igr\}_{j=1}^{N}. For each benchmark 𝒟 k \mathcal{D}_{k} with task evaluation metric ℳ \mathcal{M} , the dataset-level optimal weight is defined as: (3) θ k ∗ = arg ⁡ max θ ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] ​ 1 | 𝒟 k | ​ ∑ ( x , Y ) ∈ 𝒟 k ℳ ⁡ ( f ϕ ​ ( V θ , T ) , Y ) , 	heta_{k}^{*}=\arg\max_{	heta\in[0,1]}\; rac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{k}|}\sum_{(x,\,Y)\in\mathcal{D}_{k}}\mathcal{M}\!\left(f_{\phi}\!\left(V_{	heta},\,Tight),\,Yight), where Y Y is the ground-