Visual diversity in token selection has been approached in two main ways, each with a distinct failure mode. Max-min diversity methods ( Alvar et al., 2025 ) prioritize tokens that are maximally distant from the current selection, which makes them sensitive to outliers: a single token lying in a visually uninformative region can dominate the selection simply by being far from others. Alternatively, coverage-based methods favor tokens that best represent dense groups of similar visual patches, which tends to over-allocate budget to large homogeneous regions (e.g., background) and under-represent compact foreground structures rich in semantic detail. Inspired by the facility location problem in combinatorial optimization, we propose a selection criterion that avoids both failure modes. In the facility location analogy, each unselected token is a “client” that must be served by the nearest selected token (“facility”). Adding a new token is only beneficial if it meaningfully improves coverage for clients that are currently underserved. This naturally penalizes both redundant selections and outliers. Formally, let 𝐊 ∈ ℝ N × N \mathbf{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{N	imes N} denote the cosine similarity matrix of the visual tokens, where each entry is defined as: (12) K i ​ j = v i ⋅ v j ‖ v i ‖ ​ ‖ v j ‖ . K_{ij}= rac{v_{i}\cdot v_{j}}{\|v_{i}\|\,\|v_{j}\|}. Given the already-selected set S S , the Visual Marginal Coverage Gain (VMCG) of a candidate token v i v_{i} is: (13) Div ⁡ ( v i ∣ S ) \displaystyle\mathrm{Div}(v_{i}\mid S) = γ ⋅ max ⁡ ( 0 , K i ​ i − max s ∈ S ⁡ K s ​ i ) \displaystyle=\gamma\cdot\max\!\left(0,\,K_{ii}-\max_{s\in S}K_{si}ight) + ( 1 − γ ) ⋅ ∑ v j ∉ S , j ≠ i max ( 0 , K i ​ j − max s ∈ S K s ​ j ) . \displaystyle+(1{-}\gamma)\cdot\!\!\sum_{v_{j}
